# Caldera Breaker — sculpting and rigging brief

You are sculpting and rigging the Caldera Breaker, the slow, armored siege of the Slag
— a wide, top-heavy obsidian biped with a brick-slab torso on two short thick legs that
walks knuckled over two enormous fused ram-forearms and grinds through what it reaches —
as a 3D voxel model with a rig a game can pose at runtime. Build it with `voxel-anim`
(painting opaque cells one operation at a time) as a rigged model that reads
unmistakably as this heavy obsidian thing, trudges believably on its two legs, and rams
on demand. There is no target model to copy.

This test measures creativity and craft, not instruction-following. The brief fixes
what the Breaker is and how it must move; it deliberately does not hand you a parts
list, joint placements, or pose angles — working out the pieces a trudging, ramming
creature needs, where they attach, and how they articulate is the test. Invent the rig.

## The volume and coordinate system

- The volume is **{{voxel.width}} wide (x) × {{voxel.height}} tall (y) × {{voxel.depth}} deep (z)**, in opaque voxels. It
  starts empty.
- x runs across the Breaker, `0`–`{{voxel.maxX}}`. y runs up, `0` (bottom, the ground) to `{{voxel.maxY}}`
  (top). z runs front-to-back, `0`–`{{voxel.maxZ}}`.
- Forward is +z: the Breaker faces toward `z = {{voxel.maxZ}}` (the front) at rest, the ram-forearms
  reaching forward. Up is +y.
- Build the Breaker symmetric about the lengthwise vertical centerplane — the two legs
  and the two ram-forearms mirror left/right, and the torso, ram-head, and chest seams
  are centered on it.
- The Breaker is deliberately **wide and top-heavy** — a broad slab torso far broader
  than its hips, hunched forward over its rams, riding on two short thick legs. It is
  the most armored thing on the field; nothing about it should read as low and long,
  spindly, or lightly built.
- Each part is sculpted separately with `voxel-anim --part <name>`, in this same
  volume's coordinates, positioned where the part sits on the assembled Breaker (a leg
  already under its corner of the torso, a ram-forearm already reaching forward).

## Palette

Use only these opaque colors:

| Role | Hex |
| --- | --- |
| Body — obsidian | `#241f2b` |
| Deep recess, shadowed seam — obsidian dark | `#16131b` |
| Accent plate (the accent region) | `#4a4358` |
| Acid glow — the chest seams | `#9ede3a` |

Do **not** use steel `#c9ced6` or violet `#b56bff` anywhere on the model. A game
paints those on at run time (see *The accent region*); baking them in defeats it.

## What the Breaker is (and what is yours to invent)

Fixed — the Breaker must read unmistakably as all of these:

- A **wide, top-heavy slab torso** of obsidian — a brick-like block far broader than
  the hips, hunched slightly forward, carried high over the legs. Its shadowed seams
  and recesses are obsidian dark.
- **Two short, thick legs**, one under each side, that carry the heavy mass and trudge
  (see the animations). They read as stout and armored — the legs of something built
  for weight, not speed.
- **Two enormous fused ram-forearms** it walks slightly knuckled over — these are
  shields and battering rams, **not** hands. It has **no fingers and no weapon**: the
  forearms end blunt.
- A **low fused ram-head** sunk between the shoulders with **no visible neck**.
- **Acid-green seams cracking across the slab chest**, and no other glow anywhere on
  the model. There are **no eyes**: those cracked chest seams are the Breaker's whole
  glow signature.

The Breaker carries **no weapon, no fingers, and no tools** — the ram-forearms are its
whole armament. It is the wide, top-heavy, armored one: nothing about it reads as low,
long, fast, spindly, or massive.

Everything else is yours to invent — the exact silhouette and proportions of the torso,
legs, ram-forearms, and ram-head, how the chest seams crack across the slab, and how you
break the Breaker into rig parts. Nothing here prescribes a shape; the test rewards a
bold, characterful design that is unmistakably the Breaker and animates convincingly.

## The accent region — a hard contract

A game recolors this model at run time to show the Breaker's **tier**, by finding every
voxel of one reserved color and repainting it. That reserved color is the **accent
plate** color `#4a4358`, and it is what makes the contract work:

- Sculpt the **chest-slab plates** AND **the plates on the outer face of both
  ram-forearms** — and only those — in `#4a4358`.
- Use `#4a4358` **nowhere else** on the model. Not a stray voxel, not a highlight, not
  a seam. Every voxel of it is treated as an accent plate.
- The accent must be **coherent contiguous plates** — the chest as one plate group,
  each forearm's outer face as another, not scattered flecks — and it must be clearly
  **visible from more than one angle**, so a player reads a Breaker's tier at a glance
  across the battlefield.

Everything else — the torso body, the recesses, the legs, the ram-head, the chest
seams — keeps its own color and is never repainted. Get this wrong and every Breaker in
the game looks the same tier.

## The required animations — the fixed contract

`rig.json` is pre-seeded with two required animation declarations by name (you author
the motion). Author each with `voxel-anim define-animation` then `add-keyframe`,
choosing the period and each key's `--interp` (`constant`/`linear`/`bezier` or
`ease-in`/`ease-out`/`ease-in-out`, with optional `--in-handle`/`--out-handle`) so the
motion has weight — the Breaker is heavy and armored, so its motion is slow and
deliberate, never the light snap of a small creature.

- **`move`** — the trudge (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The two short
  thick legs plant and the heavy mass rocks side to side over each planted foot, so it
  reads as a slow, armored machine hauling its weight, not gliding or flailing. Authored
  in place: the body does not translate across the scene — the leg cycle alone carries
  the stride (played on its own the planted foot slides straight back under the body,
  treadmill-style, then swings forward), and a game supplies the real travel. The
  ram-forearms stay carried low and forward as it walks.
- **`attack`** — the ram (a game-triggered playable, `loop = true`). The Breaker rears
  up and drives both fused ram-forearms down and forward into what it is grinding
  through, then recovers to its stance before looping — a heavy commit, a recovery. The
  whole top-heavy mass throws behind the ram; this is a creature with no weapon slamming
  its forearms into a target. The feet stay planted.

You may add your own extra parts, joints, or animations on top of this (for example a
slow heave of the chest at rest), but you must not drop or contradict the required
`move` and `attack` animations, by those names.

## Working the tool

The only way to place a voxel and edit the rig is the `voxel-anim` binary already on
your `PATH`. Define your own parts with `define-part` (giving each a parent and a
pivot), sculpt each by selecting it with `--part <name>`, set pivots with `set-pivot`,
place joints with `define-joint`, and author the two animations' keyframes with
`define-animation`/`add-keyframe`. Run `voxel-anim --help` for the available operations
(setting and clearing single voxels, filling and stroking boxes, 3D lines, spheres, and
a mirror plane), the rig subcommands, and the animation subcommands, and `voxel-anim
<operation> --help` for each one's exact flags. Call `voxel-anim` once per operation;
run `voxel-anim render` and read `parts/<part>.png` (and the assembled `scene/*.png`
previews) between calls to judge each part against this brief — that each leg seats
under its own corner of the torso, the ram-forearms hang from the shoulders and reach
the ground, the ram-head sits sunk between them, and the animations read with the right
weight. Run `voxel-anim render` before you finish so it emits the per-part `.glb`
geometry your result is built from — an unrendered part scores as empty (`voxel-anim
render --component <part>` renders one part; `voxel-anim render --time <ms> --animation
<name>` renders the model posed at that instant to check the motion).
